@inject ApiMetadata Api
@* Toolbar's Overview, written by hand after Button's: the editor toolbar first, then what moves and
   when, the regions, form posts, other content, OnClick, wrapping, and how it works without script.
   Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A Toolbar stays one row: what doesn't fit moves into a … menu at the end, where each control becomes a menu item with the same link, form post or <Code>OnClick</Code>. It works with JavaScript off, so a phone with a slow connection gets the same toolbar.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">An editor toolbar</Heading>
<TextBlock>Put <Code>ToolbarButton</Code>s, <Code>ToolbarIconButton</Code>s, <Code>ToolbarButtonGroup</Code>s and <Code>ToolbarMenu</Code>s in it, the toolbar's own <Anchor Href="docs/components/button">Button</Anchor>, <Anchor Href="docs/components/icon-button">IconButton</Anchor>, <Anchor Href="docs/components/button-group">ButtonGroup</Anchor> and <Anchor Href="docs/components/menu">Menu</Anchor> with the same parameters, and narrow the window: they move into the … menu, a group as one. Other controls throw there: put them in a <Code>ToolbarItem</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-editor" />
<TextBlock>Its buttons are <Code>Ghost</Code>, <Code>Neutral</Code> and small unless they set their own <Code>Variant</Code>, <Code>Accent</Code> and <Code>Size</Code>, so one solid button stands out.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">What moves first</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Priority</Code> on an item (higher stays longer) and <Code>Overflow</Code>: <Code>Never</Code> for the main action, <Code>Always</Code> for what only the menu needs.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-priority" />
<TextBlock>With the same priority, controls move from the middle of the toolbar outward: the start from its end, then the end from its start, so the main action at the far end goes last. A <Code>ToolbarSeparator</Code> goes when there's nothing left on one side of it.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Start, center and end</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Position</Code> on an item to put it in the middle or at the end; items are at the start by default. <Code>ToolbarText</Code> is text that stays, such as a document's name.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-regions" />
<TextBlock>The center stays centered, so the start and the end each get half of the room around it, and the wider of the two gives up its controls first.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Posting a form</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Submit</Code>, <Code>Name</Code> and <Code>Value</Code> on its buttons as on a Button: in the … menu they post the same, with JavaScript off too. Put the form around the toolbar.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-bulk-actions" />

<Heading Level="2">Fields, a SegmentedControl and a Menu</Heading>
<TextBlock>Put other content in a <Code>ToolbarItem</Code> with a <Code>Width</Code>, and give it <Code>MenuContent</Code> for what stands in for it in the menu; without that, it stays in the bar.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-filters" />
<TextBlock>A <Code>ToolbarMenu</Code> becomes a submenu. The state's <Code>MenuRadioGroup</Code> posts under its own name, <Code>state</Code>: the <Anchor Href="docs/components/segmented-control">SegmentedControl</Anchor>'s radios post with the form too, even while it's in the menu.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Running C# on click</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>OnClick</Code> on its buttons on interactive pages; it runs from the bar or the menu.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-on-click" />

<Heading Level="2">Toggle buttons</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>@@bind-Pressed</Code> on its buttons for tools that turn on and off. In the … menu they're checkbox items that toggle the same.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-toggles" />

<Heading Level="2">Icons before the menu</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>CollapseLabels</Code> to drop the text of buttons and menus with a <Code>StartIcon</Code> before anything moves into the … menu: they show only their icons, all at once, and the text stays for screen readers and as the tooltip. Narrow the window: once the icons alone don't fit, items move into the menu as before. One with <Code>Overflow="ToolbarOverflow.Never"</Code>, such as Publish here, keeps its text.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-collapse-labels" />

<Heading Level="2">Wrapping instead</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Wrap</Code> for a toolbar where everything must show: it wraps onto more rows, with no menu.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-wrap" />

<Heading Level="2">In an app header</Heading>
<TextBlock>Put the header's actions in a Toolbar with <Code>Grow</Code>, after the brand, at its end: on phones they move into the … menu. <Anchor Href="demos/toolbar/app-header">See it run</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@InHeader</Code>
<TextBlock>The header stays the page's banner, and its navigation links belong in a <Anchor Href="docs/components/site-nav">SiteNav</Anchor>: a toolbar is for controls.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Without script</Heading>
<TextBlock>The server works out the width at which each control moves, from its label and icons in Inter, the library's font, and the toolbar shows the result with container queries. The script measures and corrects it: for Inter the two agree, so nothing moves when it arrives.</TextBlock>
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem>Give a <Code>ToolbarItem</Code> a <Code>Width</Code>, so the server can allow for it.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>A theme with another font can be a few pixels out until the script measures.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>A toolbar takes its width from its container, as a container query needs: in a row, or in a box that takes its width from its content, give it <Code>Grow</Code> or a width.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>The toolbar draws its items itself, in the order they reach it: one inside a component of your own (an <Code>AuthorizeView</Code>), or one an <Code>@@if</Code> adds on an interactive page, comes after the rest of its position. A cascading value written around an item inside the toolbar doesn't reach it.</ListItem>
</List>

@if (OverviewParts.Of(Component, Api).Count > 0)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Parts</Heading>
}
<OverviewParts Component="Component" />

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <Toolbar Label="Editor">
            <ToolbarButtonGroup Label="History">
                <ToolbarIconButton Icon="Lucide.Undo2" Label="Undo" />
                <ToolbarIconButton Icon="Lucide.Redo2" Label="Redo" />
            </ToolbarButtonGroup>
            <ToolbarSeparator />
            <ToolbarButton StartIcon="Lucide.Link" Priority="1">Link</ToolbarButton>
            @* The main action, at the end, never moves into the … menu; Print is only ever there. *@
            <ToolbarButton Position="ToolbarPosition.End" Overflow="ToolbarOverflow.Never" Accent="Accent.Primary" Variant="ButtonVariant.Solid">Publish</ToolbarButton>
            <ToolbarButton StartIcon="Lucide.Printer" Overflow="ToolbarOverflow.Always">Print</ToolbarButton>
        </Toolbar>
        """;

    private const string InHeader = """
        <AppHeader>
            <SidebarToggle />
            <AppBrand Href="/" Icon="Lucide.Mail" Label="Acme Mail" />
            <Toolbar Label="Mail actions" Grow>
                <ToolbarIconButton Icon="Lucide.Search" Label="Search mail" Position="ToolbarPosition.End" />
                <ToolbarIconButton Icon="Lucide.Bell" Label="Notifications" Position="ToolbarPosition.End" />
                <ToolbarButton Position="ToolbarPosition.End" Priority="1" Accent="Accent.Primary" Variant="ButtonVariant.Solid">Compose</ToolbarButton>
            </Toolbar>
        </AppHeader>
        """;
}
